### 2\. Snort combinations for website investigations

These are useful monitoring combinations.

| Combination          | What you investigate                                  |
| -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| Snort + Burp Suite   | Suspicious HTTP requests and responses                |
| Snort + Wireshark    | Network behaviour during testing                      |
| Snort + Nmap         | Unexpected exposed services                           |
| Snort + DNS analysis | DNS resolution and unexpected destinations            |
| Snort + TLS analysis | TLS handshakes and connection errors                  |
| Snort + OWASP ZAP    | Traffic generated during authorised security tests    |
| Snort + server logs  | Correlation of network events with application events |

### 3\. Custom Snort rules for website security monitoring

Create a dedicated rules file:

```bash
sudo nano /etc/snort/bugbounty.rules
```

The following Snort 3 examples monitor potentially interesting HTTP behaviour.

A. Detect requests to administrative paths

```snort
alert http any any -> any any (
    msg:"BUGBOUNTY - Admin path requested";
    flow:established,to_server;
    http_uri;
    content:"/admin";
    sid:2000001;
    rev:1;
)
```

B. Detect unusual HTTP methods

```snort
alert http any any -> any any (
    msg:"BUGBOUNTY - Unusual HTTP method";
    flow:established,to_server;
    http_method;
    content:"TRACE";
    sid:2000002;
    rev:1;
)
```

C. Detect possible directory traversal patterns

```snort
alert http any any -> any any (
    msg:"BUGBOUNTY - Traversal pattern observed";
    flow:established,to_server;
    http_uri;
    content:"../";
    sid:2000003;
    rev:1;
)
```

D. Detect HTTP server errors

```snort
alert http any any -> any any (
    msg:"BUGBOUNTY - HTTP server error";
    flow:established,to_client;
    http_stat_code;
    content:"500";
    sid:2000004;
    rev:1;
)
```

E. Detect suspiciously large HTTP headers

```snort
alert http any any -> any any (
    msg:"BUGBOUNTY - Large HTTP header line";
    http_max_header_line:>1024;
    sid:2000005;
    rev:1;
)
```

These are monitoring signatures, not vulnerability confirmations. For example, visiting `/admin` does not prove an access-control vulnerability.

Snort 3 supports separate HTTP buffers for URIs, methods, headers, cookies, request bodies and response information. Snort 3 Rule Writing Guide +1

### 4\. Combine Snort with bug bounty vulnerability categories

This is where your testing becomes more useful.

| Vulnerability               | Snort's contribution                        | Main testing tool            |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| IDOR                        | Observe HTTP requests                       | Burp Suite                   |
| Broken access control       | Monitor access attempts                     | Burp Suite                   |
| SQL injection               | Detect selected suspicious request patterns | Burp Suite / manual testing  |
| Cross-site scripting (XSS)  | Observe request patterns                    | Burp Suite                   |
| SSRF                        | Monitor unexpected outbound connections     | Burp + server-side telemetry |
| Authentication flaws        | Observe authentication traffic              | Burp Suite                   |
| Session vulnerabilities     | Monitor cookie behaviour                    | Burp Suite                   |
| Directory traversal         | Detect suspicious URI patterns              | Burp Suite                   |
| Security misconfiguration   | Observe HTTP headers and responses          | Burp Suite / ZAP             |
| Information disclosure      | Monitor responses                           | Burp Suite                   |
| API vulnerabilities         | Inspect API request patterns                | Burp Suite                   |
| File upload vulnerabilities | Observe upload traffic                      | Burp Suite                   |

Important: Snort cannot establish that an IDOR, SQL injection or SSRF vulnerability exists merely by detecting a matching packet. You need to verify the application's behaviour.

